use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Announcement import Announcement
from ../../../behaviors/KeyboardShortcuts import KeyboardShortcuts
from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence

export ui-component Toast:
    description:
        A toast is a short message that pops up in a corner of the screen to
        say something has happened, such as "Message sent", and goes away by
        itself after a few seconds. It doesn't interrupt what the user is
        doing.

    behaviors:
        - @{Announcement}
        - @{Presence}
        - @{KeyboardShortcuts}

    content:
        A title, and optionally a line of description. It can have one action
        button, such as Undo, and a close button.

    kinds:
        A toast can be marked as information, success, a warning, an error, or
        loading. Each kind looks different, and not only by colour. The
        component can add kinds of its own.

    timing:
        Each toast stays for long enough to read, then goes. How long depends on
        its kind, and the component can change it for any toast. A loading
        toast stays until it's changed. A toast with an action stays longer, so
        there is time to reach it.

    pausing:
        While the pointer is over the toasts, or focus is in them, none of them
        go away. The countdown also stops while the page is hidden, such as
        when the user switches to another tab, so nothing is missed. The
        component can pause and resume them too.

    updating:
        A toast can be changed while it's showing, such as a loading toast
        turning into "Saved" or "Couldn't save" when a task finishes. The
        component can follow a task and do this for it. The component can also
        remove a toast early.

    stacking:
        New toasts join a stack in the corner or edge the component chooses.
        There is a limit on how many show at once. The rest wait their turn,
        and more urgent ones, such as errors, go first.

    announcing:
        Assistive tools read out each toast when it appears, as Announcement
        describes, without moving focus. Whether errors interrupt what is being
        read is up to the chosen library.

    focus:
        A toast never takes focus. A keyboard shortcut moves focus to the
        toasts, so their buttons can be reached, and assistive tools are told
        what the shortcut is. When focus leaves them, it goes back where it was.

    closing:
        The close button, and Escape while focus is on a toast, close it
        straight away. Whether it can be swiped away is up to the chosen
        library. The component is told when a toast has gone.

    nonGoals:
        - Nothing important lives only in a toast. It may go before the user
            sees it, so its action is also available elsewhere
        - Not for questions that need an answer. That's a Dialog with an alert
        - Not attached to anything on the page, and not a banner that stays
            until closed
